e mandatory restart protocol and was bounded (single node, single restart). The lifecycle diff shows no unexpected residue beyond expected reboot artifacts (/tmp/reboot.log, new systemd-private tmp dirs tied to the new boot, changed sshd/chronyd PIDs) which are normal consequences of a reboot, not executor-caused clutter. No failed services, no damaged package state (dnf reports healthy in both snapshots), no unnecessary or unrelated mutations observed.","evidence_ids":["cmd-efcfd37ecafd4cb0","cmd-25eb5794e97d4db8","cmd-0f5ef427de67485d","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"The executor correctly identified curl-7.76.1-31.el9.x86_64, installed the versionlock dnf plugin, and added a versionlock pinning curl to its exact current version. It then proved the lock's effectiveness with both a targeted no-op check and a full 210-package dry-run update that excluded curl (while the separate libcurl package was still eligible, as expected). The mandatory single-node restart protocol was also completed: a healthy pre-restart baseline, an acknowledged reboot with a brief unreachable window, a changed boot identity, and confirmation that both the curl version and the versionlock entry survived the reboot. Lifecycle snapshots show no unexplained residue, failed services, or package-health degradation beyond expected reboot artifacts.","limitations":["Persistence of the lock was only verified immediately after one reboot; longer-term persistence (e.g., across a real dnf upgrade actually being run, not just a dry-run) was not tested.","The dry-run update evidence confirms curl is excluded from the transaction plan but does not execute a real update to observe post-update state, which is an acceptable and lower-risk way to demonstrate the lock without mutating 210 packages unnecessarily."]},"ttft_ms":4128,"type":"result","duration_ms":31936,"uuid":"aa5a87bf-642f-4b71-8022-5913de0c5a0d","ttft_stream_ms":877,